%I A399898 #17 Sep 23 2026 23:57:32
%S A399898 1,6,15,19,22,26,33,41,54,59,61,65,68,70,73,90,101,103,110,114,119,
%T A399898 130,135,150,153,163,170,179,185,192,198,223,240,242,256,268,274,291,
%U A399898 299,310,315,318,338,361,378,385,389,393,399,409,420,437,452,477,487,491
%N A399898 Partial sums missed by the grasshopper infinite barrycade (recursively defined family of permutations of the positive integers having pairwise disjoint sets of partial sums).
%C A399898 A barrycade is a collection of rows whose sets of partial sums are pairwise disjoint. In the infinite version considered here, each row is a permutation of the positive integers.
%C A399898 The grasshopper barrycade is constructed row by row. Suppose some initial part of the current row has sum s, and let k be the smallest positive integer not yet used in that row. Choose the smallest unused positive integer k' != k such that neither s+k' nor s+k'+k is a partial sum occurring in an earlier row. Append k', followed by k, and repeat this procedure indefinitely. The resulting rows are permutations of the positive integers and have pairwise disjoint sets of partial sums. However, unlike the precise grasshopper construction A399900, they do not cover all positive integers by partial sums. This sequence lists, in increasing order, the positive integers which never occur as a partial sum in any row of the grasshopper barrycade.
%C A399898 It is conjectured that this sequence is infinite (Dębski et al.).
%C A399898 The spelling "barrycade" is due to Richard Guy, who used it instead of "barricade" in honor of Barry Cipra, who prompted him to study the problem.
%H A399898 Sean A. Irvine, <a href="/A399898/b399898.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..5000</a>
%H A399898 M. Dębski, J. Grytczuk, P. Naroski, B. Pawlik, J. Przybyło and M. Śleszyńska-Nowak, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.18476">Finite and infinite barrycades</a>, arXiv:2609.18476 [math.CO], 2026.
%F A399898 The sequence is the increasing complement in the positive integers of the union of the sets of partial sums of all rows of the grasshopper infinite barrycade.
%F A399898 a(1) = min { m >= 1 : A399899(m)=0 }; for n >= 2, a(n) = min { m > a(n-1) : A399899(m)=0 }.
%e A399898 The first row begins psi_1 = (2,1,4,3,6,5,8,7,10,9,...), so its partial sums begin 2,3,7,10,16,21,29,36,46,55,...
%e A399898 The partial sum 1 occurs in no row of the grasshopper barrycade and is therefore the first term of the sequence. The next missed partial sums are 6, 15, 19, 22, ...
%t A399898 A399898[n_] := Module[{u,s,p,init,step,extend,covered,missingQ,x=0,a={}},covered[0,_]=False;covered[r_,y_]:=AnyTrue[Range[r],(init[#];extend[#,y];MemberQ[p[#],y])&];step[r_]:=Module[{k=1,q=1},While[MemberQ[u[r],k],k++];While[MemberQ[u[r],q]||q==k||covered[r-1,s[r]+q]||covered[r-1,s[r]+q+k],q++];Scan[(AppendTo[u[r],#];s[r]+=#;AppendTo[p[r],s[r]])&,{q,k}]];init[r_]:=If[!ValueQ[s[r]],u[r]={};s[r]=0;p[r]={};step[r]];extend[r_,y_]:=(init[r];While[s[r]<y,step[r]]);missingQ[y_]:=Module[{r=1},While[True,init[r];If[First[u[r]]>y,Return[True]];extend[r,y];If[MemberQ[p[r],y],Return[False]];r++]];While[Length[a]<n,x++;If[missingQ[x],AppendTo[a,x]]];a]
%Y A399898 Cf. A399897-A399909 for other sequences arising from infinite barrycade constructions.
%K A399898 nonn,new
%O A399898 1,2
%A A399898 _Bartlomiej Pawlik_, Sep 16 2026
